{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CJz9wf9Y8ktgrVdmYToS6Y","type":"tool_result","content":"789\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/control-plane-components.md ===\n790\t# Concept: Control-plane components\n791\t\n792\t**Home:** Chapter 3 §2 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n793\t\n794\t## The cluster shape\n795\t\n796\t\"A Kubernetes cluster consists of a control plane plus a set of worker machines, called\n797\tnodes, that run containerized applications. Every cluster needs at least one worker node\n798\tin order to run Pods. The worker nodes host the Pods that are the components of the\n799\tapplication workload. The control plane manages the worker nodes and the Pods in the\n800\tcluster. In production environments, the control plane usually runs across multiple\n801\tcomputers and a cluster usually runs multiple nodes, providing fault tolerance and high\n802\tavailability.\" [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]\n803\t\n804\tThe control plane's components \"make global decisions about the cluster — scheduling, for\n805\texample — as well as detecting and responding to cluster events, such as starting up a\n806\tnew Pod when a controller's declared replica count is unsatisfied.\"\n807\t\n808\t## The five\n809\t\n810\t| Component | One job | Optional? |\n811\t|---|---|---|\n812\t| **kube-apiserver** | Exposes the Kubernetes HTTP API. **The front end for the control plane.** Scales horizontally by adding instances. | no |\n813\t| **etcd** | Consistent, highly-available key value store; backing store for **all** cluster data. | no |\n814\t| **kube-scheduler** | Watches for newly created Pods with no assigned node and **selects** a node. | no |\n815\t| **kube-controller-manager** | Runs controller processes. **One binary, one process.** | no |\n816\t| **cloud-controller-manager** | Embeds cloud-specific control logic; links the cluster to a cloud provider's API. | **yes** |\n817\t\n818\t## Two boundaries that must not drift\n819\t\n820\t**1. The scheduler selects; it does not start.** \"The scheduler selects a node and records\n821\tthat choice. It does not start anything.\" It \"notifies the API server about its decision,\n822\tin a process called binding\" [source: k8s-docs-kube-scheduler-2026-08-23]. The kubelet on\n823\tthe chosen node starts the containers. Chapter 3 calls the contrary belief — that the\n824\tscheduler contacts the node — \"the highest-value wrong answer on the page, because the\n825\tbelief behind it is genuinely widespread.\"\n826\t\n827\t**2. One binary, one process.** \"Logically, each controller is a separate process, but to\n828\treduce complexity, they are all compiled into a single binary and run in a single\n829\tprocess.\" Chapter 3's ⚠ Navigational Hazards spells out the failure mode: candidates\n830\tanswer \"one process per controller,\" \"one container per controller,\" or \"one Pod per\n831\tcontroller.\" **All three are wrong for the same reason: there is no per-controller unit of\n832\tany kind.**\n833\t\n834\t## etcd — the two words worth unpacking\n835\t\n836\tThe official description is terse. Chapter 3 unpacks two words and marks the unpacking as\n837\t**its own, not the documentation's**: *\"all cluster data\"* means every object you created,\n838\tevery object the system created, every piece of state the cluster knows about; and\n839\t*\"consistent\"* \"is what lets independent components reading the same shared state work\n840\tfrom the same picture of the world rather than from private, drifting copies.\"\n841\t\n842\tThe ⚓ callout is the reusable payload: other components \"can be killed, restarted, or\n843\treplaced and rebuild their working picture from what etcd holds. etcd itself holds the\n844\tthing that cannot be rebuilt from anywhere else.\"\n845\t\n846\t## 🪢 The mnemonic (verbatim — it is a sorting rule, not an acronym)\n847\t\n848\t> \"Five components, and they sort themselves. **Three named `kube-`** (apiserver,\n849\t> scheduler, controller-manager) are the core Kubernetes control-plane processes. **One\n850\t> named `cloud-`** (cloud-controller-manager) is the optional bridge to somebody else's\n851\t> infrastructure. **One with no prefix at all**, etcd, because it isn't Kubernetes\n852\t> software; it's a general-purpose datastore that Kubernetes uses.\"\n853\t\n854\t## Figure\n855\t\n856\t`ch03-fig01-control-plane-and-node-components` — the whole census on one page. **Solid\n857\tborder = always present; dashed border = optional.** The caption is load-bearing: \"The\n858\tdashes are worth as many exam points as the names.\"\n859\t\n860\t## Downstream obligations\n861\t\n862\t| Chapter | Obligation |\n863\t|---|---|\n864\t| Ch 7 | The scheduler's actual selection algorithm. Ch 3 deliberately withholds it. |\n865\t| Ch 8 | etcd backup and restore; the auth → authz → admission gates a request passes. |\n866\t| Ch 12 | Secrets live in etcd — why encryption at rest is a separate decision. |\n867\t\n868\t## Related\n869\t\n870\t[[node-components]] · [[api-server-hub]] · [[optional-components]] · [[control-loop]]\n871\t=== END WRITE ===\n872\t\n873\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/node-components.md ==="}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7e21906-b550-465e-885a-6133312befb8","uuid":"e03713d4-ee1b-482e-b008-c6cc4f687ccd","timestamp":"2026-08-24T14:51:23.134Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-03\\kb-manifest.md","content":"=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/control-plane-components.md ===\n# Concept: Control-plane components\n\n**Home:** Chapter 3 §2 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n\n## The cluster shape\n\n\"A Kubernetes cluster consists of a control plane plus a set of worker machines, called\nnodes, that run containerized applications. Every cluster needs at least one worker node\nin order to run Pods. The worker nodes host the Pods that are the components of the\napplication workload. The control plane manages the worker nodes and the Pods in the\ncluster. In production environments, the control plane usually runs across multiple\ncomputers and a cluster usually runs multiple nodes, providing fault tolerance and high\navailability.\" [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]\n\nThe control plane's components \"make global decisions about the cluster — scheduling, for\nexample — as well as detecting and responding to cluster events, such as starting up a\nnew Pod when a controller's declared replica count is unsatisfied.\"\n\n## The five\n\n| Component | One job | Optional? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **kube-apiserver** | Exposes the Kubernetes HTTP API. **The front end for the control plane.** Scales horizontally by adding instances. | no |\n| **etcd** | Consistent, highly-available key value store; backing store for **all** cluster data. | no |\n| **kube-scheduler** | Watches for newly created Pods with no assigned node and **selects** a node. | no |\n| **kube-controller-manager** | Runs controller processes. **One binary, one process.** | no |\n| **cloud-controller-manager** | Embeds cloud-specific control logic; links the cluster to a cloud provider's API. | **yes** |\n\n## Two boundaries that must not drift\n\n**1. The scheduler selects; it does not start.** \"The scheduler selects a node and records\nthat choice. It does not start anything.\" It \"notifies the API server about its decision,\nin a process called binding\" [source: k8s-docs-kube-scheduler-2026-08-23]. The kubelet on\nthe chosen node starts the containers. Chapter 3 calls the contrary belief — that the\nscheduler contacts the node — \"the highest-value wrong answer on the page, because the\nbelief behind it is genuinely widespread.\"\n\n**2. One binary, one process.** \"Logically, each controller is a separate process, but to\nreduce complexity, they are all compiled into a single binary and run in a single\nprocess.\" Chapter 3's ⚠ Navigational Hazards spells out the failure mode: candidates\nanswer \"one process per controller,\" \"one container per controller,\" or \"one Pod per\ncontroller.\" **All three are wrong for the same reason: there is no per-controller unit of\nany kind.**\n\n## etcd — the two words worth unpacking\n\nThe official description is terse. Chapter 3 unpacks two words and marks the unpacking as\n**its own, not the documentation's**: *\"all cluster data\"* means every object you created,\nevery object the system created, every piece of state the cluster knows about; and\n*\"consistent\"* \"is what lets independent components reading the same shared state work\nfrom the same picture of the world rather than from private, drifting copies.\"\n\nThe ⚓ callout is the reusable payload: other components \"can be killed, restarted, or\nreplaced and rebuild their working picture from what etcd holds. etcd itself holds the\nthing that cannot be rebuilt from anywhere else.\"\n\n## 🪢 The mnemonic (verbatim — it is a sorting rule, not an acronym)\n\n> \"Five components, and they sort themselves. **Three named `kube-`** (apiserver,\n> scheduler, controller-manager) are the core Kubernetes control-plane processes. **One\n> named `cloud-`** (cloud-controller-manager) is the optional bridge to somebody else's\n> infrastructure. **One with no prefix at all**, etcd, because it isn't Kubernetes\n> software; it's a general-purpose datastore that Kubernetes uses.\"\n\n## Figure\n\n`ch03-fig01-control-plane-and-node-components` — the whole census on one page. **Solid\nborder = always present; dashed border = optional.** The caption is load-bearing: \"The\ndashes are worth as many exam points as the names.\"\n\n## Downstream obligations\n\n| Chapter | Obligation |\n|---|---|\n| Ch 7 | The scheduler's actual selection algorithm. Ch 3 deliberately withholds it. |\n| Ch 8 | etcd backup and restore; the auth → authz → admission gates a request passes. |\n| Ch 12 | Secrets live in etcd — why encryption at rest is a separate decision. |\n\n## Related\n\n[[node-components]] · [[api-server-hub]] · [[optional-components]] · [[control-loop]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/node-components.md ===","numLines":85,"startLine":789,"totalLines":1696}}}
