{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0124NsfA2sXWTZhhoFuQ2dfv","type":"tool_result","content":"1290\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/label-selector.md ===\n1291\t# Concept: Labels and selectors — the universal join\n1292\t\n1293\t**Home:** Chapter 4 §5 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n1294\t**B3 classification:** one of nine cross-cutting themes — **originates here.**\n1295\t\n1296\t## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — canonical retrieval string)\n1297\t\n1298\t> **The label selector is the core grouping primitive in Kubernetes.** Labels are\n1299\t> selectable. Annotations are not. Nearly every question in Kubernetes of the form *\"which\n1300\t> objects does this apply to?\"* is answered by a selector over labels.\n1301\t> [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]\n1302\t\n1303\tThe middle phrase is the documentation's own title for the mechanism. **Retrieve it by\n1304\tthese words**, not by paraphrase — B3's rationale for naming a pattern applies here.\n1305\t\n1306\t## Labels\n1307\t\n1308\t> **Labels** are key/value pairs that are attached to objects… intended to be used to\n1309\t> specify identifying attributes of objects that are meaningful and relevant to users, but\n1310\t> that **do not directly imply semantics to the core system.**\n1311\t> [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]\n1312\t\n1313\tChapter 4's gloss on why the system's ignorance is the feature: \"Kubernetes does not know\n1314\twhat `tier: frontend` means. It has no built-in concept of a frontend. That ignorance is\n1315\tprecisely why labels are useful everywhere: because the system attaches no meaning to them,\n1316\tyou are free to attach yours, and the system's grouping machinery works identically\n1317\tregardless of what you meant. A label is closer to a signal flag than to a filing category.\"\n1318\t\n1319\t**Syntax, at the depth this exam tests.** Name segment **≤ 63 characters**, alphanumeric at\n1320\tboth ends. Optional prefix: a **DNS subdomain ≤ 253 characters** followed by a slash.\n1321\t**`kubernetes.io/` and `k8s.io/` are reserved for core components.** Values **≤ 63\n1322\tcharacters**, may be empty.\n1323\t\n1324\t## The two selector syntaxes\n1325\t\n1326\t| Type | Operators | Example |\n1327\t|---|---|---|\n1328\t| **Equality-based** | `=` `==` `!=` | `environment = production`, `tier != frontend` |\n1329\t| **Set-based** | `in` `notin` `exists` (+ negation) | `environment in (production, qa)`, `partition`, `!partition` |\n1330\t\n1331\tSet-based requirements are \"**more expressive**.\" **Commas AND multiple requirements\n1332\ttogether — regardless of which selector type they use.** (Chapter 4's Practice Q17 is built\n1333\ton exactly that trap: a comma makes an expression *look* set-based and tells you nothing\n1334\tabout the operators.)\n1335\t\n1336\t**The structured form** — supported by Job, Deployment, ReplicaSet, and DaemonSet:\n1337\t**\"`matchLabels` is a map of `{key, value}` pairs equivalent to a `matchExpressions` entry\n1338\twith operator `In`.\"** Exact equivalence; `matchLabels` is shorthand, not a weaker feature.\n1339\tSet-based operator vocabulary: `In`, `NotIn`, `Exists`, `DoesNotExist`.\n1340\t\n1341\t## ⚓ Worth Securing — why this shard matters more than its section\n1342\t\n1343\t> ReplicaSet, Service, NetworkPolicy, and node affinity are **not four mechanisms to\n1344\t> learn. They are one mechanism** — *describe a set by its attributes* — aimed at four"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7e21906-b550-465e-885a-6133312befb8","uuid":"dead316a-9292-4828-809b-f668a1c11212","timestamp":"2026-08-24T14:51:40.345Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-04\\kb-manifest.md","content":"=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/label-selector.md ===\n# Concept: Labels and selectors — the universal join\n\n**Home:** Chapter 4 §5 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**B3 classification:** one of nine cross-cutting themes — **originates here.**\n\n## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — canonical retrieval string)\n\n> **The label selector is the core grouping primitive in Kubernetes.** Labels are\n> selectable. Annotations are not. Nearly every question in Kubernetes of the form *\"which\n> objects does this apply to?\"* is answered by a selector over labels.\n> [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]\n\nThe middle phrase is the documentation's own title for the mechanism. **Retrieve it by\nthese words**, not by paraphrase — B3's rationale for naming a pattern applies here.\n\n## Labels\n\n> **Labels** are key/value pairs that are attached to objects… intended to be used to\n> specify identifying attributes of objects that are meaningful and relevant to users, but\n> that **do not directly imply semantics to the core system.**\n> [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]\n\nChapter 4's gloss on why the system's ignorance is the feature: \"Kubernetes does not know\nwhat `tier: frontend` means. It has no built-in concept of a frontend. That ignorance is\nprecisely why labels are useful everywhere: because the system attaches no meaning to them,\nyou are free to attach yours, and the system's grouping machinery works identically\nregardless of what you meant. A label is closer to a signal flag than to a filing category.\"\n\n**Syntax, at the depth this exam tests.** Name segment **≤ 63 characters**, alphanumeric at\nboth ends. Optional prefix: a **DNS subdomain ≤ 253 characters** followed by a slash.\n**`kubernetes.io/` and `k8s.io/` are reserved for core components.** Values **≤ 63\ncharacters**, may be empty.\n\n## The two selector syntaxes\n\n| Type | Operators | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Equality-based** | `=` `==` `!=` | `environment = production`, `tier != frontend` |\n| **Set-based** | `in` `notin` `exists` (+ negation) | `environment in (production, qa)`, `partition`, `!partition` |\n\nSet-based requirements are \"**more expressive**.\" **Commas AND multiple requirements\ntogether — regardless of which selector type they use.** (Chapter 4's Practice Q17 is built\non exactly that trap: a comma makes an expression *look* set-based and tells you nothing\nabout the operators.)\n\n**The structured form** — supported by Job, Deployment, ReplicaSet, and DaemonSet:\n**\"`matchLabels` is a map of `{key, value}` pairs equivalent to a `matchExpressions` entry\nwith operator `In`.\"** Exact equivalence; `matchLabels` is shorthand, not a weaker feature.\nSet-based operator vocabulary: `In`, `NotIn`, `Exists`, `DoesNotExist`.\n\n## ⚓ Worth Securing — why this shard matters more than its section\n\n> ReplicaSet, Service, NetworkPolicy, and node affinity are **not four mechanisms to\n> learn. They are one mechanism** — *describe a set by its attributes* — aimed at four","numLines":55,"startLine":1290,"totalLines":1658}}}
