rs retrieve it** |\n1718\t| **QoS classes** | ⚑ **ABSENT — outline-mandated, cut for lack of a snapshot** |\n1719\t| **Graceful termination** | ⚑ **ABSENT — `kb_tags` claims `pod-termination`** |\n1720\t| `pod-template` | deferred to Ch 6 — correct; the tag is aspirational |\n1721\t| `kubectl` command surface | deliberately deferred to Ch 13 / Ch 16 |\n1722\t\n1723\t---\n1724\t\n1725\t## ⚑ Book-level: the authored allocations are approaching the domain ceiling\n1726\t\n1727\tKubernetes Fundamentals is **44%** of the published exam. Four chapters sit inside it and each\n1728\tpublishes an authored allocation in a differently-worded metadata line:\n1729\t\n1730\t| Chapter | Claimed | Metadata form |\n1731\t|---|---|---|\n1732\t| Ch 2 | ~9% | **the only one that discloses \"authored allocation\" inline and source-tags it** |\n1733\t| Ch 3 | ~6% | \"authored estimate\" |\n1734\t| Ch 4 | ~6% | no caveat at all |\n1735\t| **Ch 5** | **7%** | \"This book's allocation\" — no caveat, no tag |\n1736\t| **Subtotal** | **28% of 44%** | |\n1737\t\n1738\tThat leaves **16 points for Chapter 6 alone** — larger than Chapters 3, 4, and 5 combined.\n1739\tEither the allocations need rebalancing or Chapter 6's will have to be authored to fit a\n1740\tresidue rather than derived from its content. **Settle before Chapter 6 drafts.**\n1741\t\n1742\tThis is also the concrete reason to act on Stage 13's T4 rather than defer it: four chapters\n1743\tpublish an authored number and **one** of them tells the reader it is authored. Chapter 1\n1744\tcommitted the book to that disclosure in prose. **Conform Ch 5 to Chapter 2's shape; flag\n1745\tCh 3 and Ch 4 for `reconcile.py`.** Note the competency separator also drifts three ways\n1746\t(`— competency: X` / `— X` / `(X)`); pick one.\n1747\t\n1748\t---\n1749\t\n1750\t## ⚑ Ethical-guardrail status — Chapter 5\n1751\t\n1752\t| Chapter | Guardrail #8 | Note |\n1753\t|---|---|---|\n1754\t| Ch 1 | pass | |\n1755\t| Ch 2 | pass | models the compliant 