{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FENAgywv65dkMQFzpcRBeL","type":"tool_result","content":"1021\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/cluster-scoped-resource.md ===\n1022\t# Concept: Namespaced vs cluster-scoped\n1023\t\n1024\t**Home:** Chapter 4 §3 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n1025\t**B3 classification:** one of nine cross-cutting themes — **originates here.**\n1026\t\n1027\t## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — this is the canonical retrieval string)\n1028\t\n1029\t> **Not everything lives in a namespace.** Nodes, PersistentVolumes, and StorageClasses are\n1030\t> cluster-scoped, and so are namespace objects themselves. Namespace scoping applies only\n1031\t> to namespaced objects. **Any question about who may act on a resource has to start by\n1032\t> asking which side of this boundary the resource is on.**\n1033\t> [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n1034\t\n1035\tDocumentation wording: \"Namespace-based scoping is applicable **only for namespaced\n1036\tobjects** (Deployments, Services, and so on) and **not for cluster-wide objects**, such as\n1037\tStorageClasses, Nodes, and PersistentVolumes… namespace resources are not themselves in a\n1038\tnamespace, and low-level resources such as nodes and persistent volumes are not in any\n1039\tnamespace.\"\n1040\t\n1041\t## ⚑ USE ONE PHRASE. Chapter 4 did not coin a name.\n1042\t\n1043\tB3's stated rationale for naming the absent-component pattern was that \"naming it once and\n1044\tretrieving it by name turns four gotchas into one rule.\" Chapter 4 gives this theme a\n1045\t★ Fixed Point but **no coinage.**\n1046\t\n1047\t**Until the author rules otherwise, `\"Not everything lives in a namespace\"` is the\n1048\tcanonical retrieval string.** Chapters 10, 11, and 12 should retrieve *that sentence*\n1049\trather than five paraphrases of it. If the author prefers a coined name, it must be chosen\n1050\tbefore Chapter 10 drafts.\n1051\t\n1052\t## The payoff, eight chapters out\n1053\t\n1054\tChapter 4 states the bearing and refuses to teach it: Kubernetes' permission model \"has two\n1055\trole types and two binding types, and the four combinations are **not a table to memorize.\n1056\tThey are a direct consequence of this boundary.** A permission over a namespaced resource\n1057\tcan be granted inside one namespace; a permission over a cluster-scoped resource cannot be,\n1058\tbecause there is no namespace to grant it in.\"\n1059\t\n1060\t**Chapter 12 must derive Role / ClusterRole / RoleBinding / ClusterRoleBinding from this"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7e21906-b550-465e-885a-6133312befb8","uuid":"3b89183a-d41b-4c00-9621-c1666ed61904","timestamp":"2026-08-24T14:54:52.845Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-04\\kb-manifest.md","content":"=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/cluster-scoped-resource.md ===\n# Concept: Namespaced vs cluster-scoped\n\n**Home:** Chapter 4 §3 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**B3 classification:** one of nine cross-cutting themes — **originates here.**\n\n## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — this is the canonical retrieval string)\n\n> **Not everything lives in a namespace.** Nodes, PersistentVolumes, and StorageClasses are\n> cluster-scoped, and so are namespace objects themselves. Namespace scoping applies only\n> to namespaced objects. **Any question about who may act on a resource has to start by\n> asking which side of this boundary the resource is on.**\n> [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n\nDocumentation wording: \"Namespace-based scoping is applicable **only for namespaced\nobjects** (Deployments, Services, and so on) and **not for cluster-wide objects**, such as\nStorageClasses, Nodes, and PersistentVolumes… namespace resources are not themselves in a\nnamespace, and low-level resources such as nodes and persistent volumes are not in any\nnamespace.\"\n\n## ⚑ USE ONE PHRASE. Chapter 4 did not coin a name.\n\nB3's stated rationale for naming the absent-component pattern was that \"naming it once and\nretrieving it by name turns four gotchas into one rule.\" Chapter 4 gives this theme a\n★ Fixed Point but **no coinage.**\n\n**Until the author rules otherwise, `\"Not everything lives in a namespace\"` is the\ncanonical retrieval string.** Chapters 10, 11, and 12 should retrieve *that sentence*\nrather than five paraphrases of it. If the author prefers a coined name, it must be chosen\nbefore Chapter 10 drafts.\n\n## The payoff, eight chapters out\n\nChapter 4 states the bearing and refuses to teach it: Kubernetes' permission model \"has two\nrole types and two binding types, and the four combinations are **not a table to memorize.\nThey are a direct consequence of this boundary.** A permission over a namespaced resource\ncan be granted inside one namespace; a permission over a cluster-scoped resource cannot be,\nbecause there is no namespace to grant it in.\"\n\n**Chapter 12 must derive Role / ClusterRole / RoleBinding / ClusterRoleBinding from this","numLines":40,"startLine":1021,"totalLines":1658}}}
